A||N||6||DaveL||I Dew||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||04-21-2000||03:31 PM||||I bought two new door window weatherstrips because mine are old and cracking and I'm sure that it will cause rust eventually, and also they look like snit. Dew strips is sure easier to type, especially when you have uncoordinutad fangers lik minnie. Well, I pulled the instructions off the Michigan Fiero Club's site, and it really looks like a pain. I have a few questions. Would it be easier to just remove the window glass for the procedure? If not, one step of their instructions says to undo the in-out window adjuster screws and to let the pads fall down during the process, and pick them back up with a bent coat hanger for the reinstall. Couldn't you just loosen them enough to let the window move inwards  without taking them completely off? One user claims to have removed the stops in the bottom of the door without having any problems, but I'm betting they're there for a reason. Is this advisable? This looks pretty scary, but I've read throgh some threads and see that many of you have done it. Ogre, can you come down to Fla. and do this for me? I'll give you a dollar... Lastly, do you really need a door panel remover, and can you get new star connectors at parts stores if you destroy them? On my 87 SE, I'd bet they'll come apart. Guess I'll be perusing the "Help" section at the local parts store/pocket cleaners.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-21-2000||06:46 PM||||While you need to remove a "bit" more stuff. Taking the inner door skin off and letting the window drop is likely as fast as fooling with custom tools to get those screws. When you take off the inner wipe and drop the window the screws are accessed with a standard straight shaft #2 phillips. Plus is you drop one in the door it's real easy to get out.<P>While your in there clean and grease the window hardware. I usually use "white" grease or silicone grease. spray oils aren't good enough for this. needs to be grease. Something that is light and won't gell bad when cold. <P>innner door skin:<BR>3 #3 phillips hold the arm rest. (one under decorative plug)<BR>2 #2 phillips hold the arm rest. (one under logo plug)<BR>Window crank held by clip. the stock position puts the open side of the clip toward the knob. (Slide the tool between the crank and the wear guard disk. jiggle it til it drops in the slot then push to release the clip. It can be a little tight.)<P>skin is then held by christmas trees. (Best to get a pannel lifter tool to prevent wrecking the pannel.)<P>the inner wipe is held to the door lip by some screws and a metal strip.<P>both the window crank tool(if you need it) and the panel tool are available at any parts store or sears. neither costs much and you'll use them more than once.<P>to put the window crank back on, put the clip on the crank(open side toward knob), and then push the crank on the shaft.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||04-21-2000||09:25 PM||||Yes, you can just loosen the adjuster screws enough without undoing them all the way so they fall off. When you get your special tool like Rodney's or the one I made being passed around, have the window up all the way and gently push against the glass. This gives you enough leveridge to get in there to those screws. It would be wise to replace the screws with metric ones for easier removal next time with a small wrench.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||04-24-2000||08:51 AM||||I haven't seen any info on how to release the window from the crank mechanism. How hard is it really if you have the door panel and the paper layer peeled off?||||reg||5
Z||000004||DJRice||04-24-2000||11:06 AM||||You definitely need to either bend a screwdriver or buy a removal tool from Rodney Dickman.  I did mine this weekend and it only took about 1.5hrs for both doors.<P>You might try finding a cheap screwdriver and bending it first.<P>Another alternative is set of needlenose vice grips and a small phillips screwdriver bit.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaveL||04-24-2000||03:36 PM||||I'm definitely going to get some cheap phillips heads and clamp them in a vice, and try to get the right bend in it. Sounds a lot easier than most of what I've heard. I know some where I've seen a flexible extension for a 1/4" drive that would be perfect for reinstalling the recommended hex-head screws. Doesn't anyone sell those anymore???  I ripped the two sections at the weatherstrip right by the two infamous unreachable screws with the door panel removal tool , then got the screws out with needle nose pliars, then reinstalled with a small strip of silicone in this area. Don't think it will cause a problem. Despite the years with the cracked and broken strip, the inside was very clean. But I want to do the drivers side completely.||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||04-24-2000||06:45 PM||||I was looking at the door next to me on the floor. I can't see quickly how the window drops. <P>You can gain some space by removing the interior trim and releasing the 2 inner pressure guides. That should give you about an extra 1/2 inch of space. they are held by a single 10mm bolt in each. You'll likely want to adjust these anyway once the new wipes are on so you don't bind the glass. These are the interior part that oposes the pads on the outer wipe.<P>||||reg||1
